00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:05.129
Badlands is an impressive debut film.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.429
Malick has a clear artistic vision from the
very start.

00:01:08.429 --> 00:01:12.880
Loosely based on real event, Badlands tells
the story of 25 year old Kit’s killing spree

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:26.240
and 15 year old Holly as she unquestioningly
accompanies him.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:47.080
From the very beginning we can see a unique
approach to story forming.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:52.979
I think perhaps the most significant element
of Malick’s style is his focus on the interior

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:55.509
landscape of the characters.

00:01:55.509 --> 00:02:01.030
His films concern themselves with what the
character’s are thinking and feeling, this

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:05.380
comes before any consideration of specific
plot details.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:10.560
I think his entire approach to filmmaking
is structured around communicating that internal

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.560
landscape.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:17.569
One of the tools he uses to do that, one that
features as an important part of Badlands

00:02:17.569 --> 00:03:00.629
and every one of his subsequent films

00:03:00.629 --> 00:03:12.129
is Voiceover.

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:18.870
While use of voiceover in film is often criticized
as a crutch for bad writing, Malick’s use

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:23.599
of voiceover serves to accompany, more than
to re-state plot.

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:27.489
Character’s are often musing or observing.

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:32.360
And you get the feeling that the voice over
is almost more for the character rather than

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.090
it is for the audience.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:40.330
Malick’s approach to voiceover is similar
to his approach to cinematography, it’s

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:48.849
there to observe, not to construct.

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:56.190
This interior landscape is naturally subjective
and it’s reinforced by the perspective of

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.609
the narrative.

00:03:57.609 --> 00:04:03.799
Malick stays with the characters that the
story focuses on, rarely zooming out to a

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:07.859
broader perspective to inform the audience.

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:13.760
The audience gets to know what the characters
know or care about and do, not much more.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.560
You’re there with them.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:21.729
And rarely do we even get a wide shot or an
establishing shot to ground the character

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:23.590
within the world.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:33.320
Rather the focus is on the character’s subjective
experience of the environment around them.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:38.960
The one time in Badlands where we do get a
birds eye view perspective of what’s happening

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:45.639
away from the protagonists, it’s cast in
an oddly stylized black and white montage,

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:51.190
almost as if we’re seeing from Holly’s
perspective in the future, how she imagined

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.539
things happened.

00:04:53.539 --> 00:05:02.460
And this subjectivity, is I think what leads
to Malick’s emphasis on nature and environment

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.949
as a part of the storytelling.

00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:10.300
This will become a much heavier focus and
play a larger role in later films, but it’s

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.840
very present in Badlands.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.330
The films title itself speaks to this approach
to filmmaking.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:23.979
After the character experience, the environment
is the next focus, again taking priority over

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:25.670
plot and narrative.

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:35.700
The films don’t emphasize locations, rather
they try to exist within a larger environment.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.129
Instead of INT.

00:05:37.129 --> 00:05:38.129
Coffee Shop.

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:39.129
DAY.

00:05:39.129 --> 00:05:51.180
We get open skies, flowing rivers, and vast
landscapes of skyscrapers.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:55.260
And this environment is one that includes
animal life.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:59.940
Malick’s inclusion of and focus on the animals
that incidentally surround his characters

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.740
is very unique in the narrative film world.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:11.960
I think the inclusion of these seemingly irrelevant
details are a large part of what creates the

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:16.699
immediacy and subjectivity of the perspective.

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:22.690
In our lives, in the present, we often don’t
yet know which details are relevant to our

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:23.690
“story.”

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:34.289
It’s only in hindsight that we can exclude
everything extraneous to the story we’d

00:06:34.289 --> 00:06:53.710
like to tell.

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:58.460
Malick often handles what plot does exist
in a very loose sense.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:04.340
Plot only exists in his films primarily as
an accessory to the character’s journey.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:09.610
While Badlands is perhaps his most Plot heavy
film, we still catch a glimpse Malick’s

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:18.280
future work in how he treats the films plot.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:22.470
One of those things is how He shows story
through montage.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:27.949
Instead of a traditional scene structure,
we get sections where he uses a montage of

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:31.169
moments, along with music and voiceover.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:36.949
He continues to expand his use of this technique
throughout his career, with some of his later

00:07:36.949 --> 00:07:40.919
films existing almost entirely in this mode.

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:46.060
Besides a the change in cinematography, the
other change in Malick’s style that lead

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.639
to where he is now is in the editing.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:54.530
It won’t be until The New World that we’ll
see the type of scenes that represent what

00:07:54.530 --> 00:08:02.770
I think of as “classically” Malick.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:15.680
But all the ingredients for scenes like that
one (Music, voiceover, and montage) are present

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:36.770
in his earlier films and Badlands.

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:42.510
It would just take a some additional experimentation
in the editing room to bring it to it’s

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:43.590
full form.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:48.710
Finally in Badlands we can see Malick’s
preference for naturalistic lighting.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:53.730
Badlands features many scenes shot during
“golden hour” the hours surrounding sunrise

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:55.160
and sunset.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:02.230
He will go on to shoot some of his later films
almost entirely during this time of day.With

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:08.320
Badlands Malick showed an immediate competence
and grasp of more conventional filmmaking.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.540
His career since then has become progressively
more experimental.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:18.600
Like Wes Anderson the first director I talked
about in this series, Terrance Malick is a

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:25.070
great example of how small elements of director’s
style evolve into something more pronounced

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.020
and obvious throughout their career.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:33.831
But also like Wes, we can see how the more
foundational elements of Malick’s style

00:09:33.831 --> 00:09:41.520
were present even before the obvious visual
characteristics emerge.

